---
title: "Server-side syntax highlighting"
---

Code is highlighted in Rust at render time (syntect → `tali-hl-` scope classes,
coloured by CSS), so a built page carries no highlighter and the light/dark toggle
restyles code without re-highlighting. This document pins the language coverage.

## Languages syntect bundles

```rust
pub fn highlight(code: &str, lang: Option<&str>) -> String {
    let ss = syntaxes();
    match lang.map(alias).and_then(|t| ss.find_syntax_by_token(t)) {
        Some(syntax) => classed(code, syntax, ss),
        None => html_escape(code), // an unknown language degrades to plain text
    }
}
```

```python
def entropy(p):
    """Shannon entropy in bits."""
    return -sum(x * log2(x) for x in p if x > 0)
```

## Languages the bundled set omits

Neither of these resolves in syntect's default syntax set, so both rendered as
unstyled plain text until the `bat`-curated extras were loaded. The docs use them,
so they are pinned here.

```ts
// A TypeScript fence: `ts` aliases to `typescript`.
export function mount(deck: HTMLElement, opts: DeckOptions = {}): void {
    const slides = deck.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".tali-slide");
    slides.forEach((s, i) => s.setAttribute("data-index", String(i)));
}
```

```toml
[workspace.dependencies]
syntect = { version = "5", default-features = false, features = ["default-fancy"] }
two-face = { version = "0.4", default-features = false }
```

## A language neither set has at all

PowerShell resolves in *neither* the bundled set nor the extras, so filling it needed
a grammar vendored into the repo rather than a dependency turned on. It is
[SublimeText/PowerShell](https://github.com/SublimeText/PowerShell)'s
`.sublime-syntax` (MIT), and the file format is the whole reason for that choice:
syntect loads `.sublime-syntax` only, so the more obvious upstream, Microsoft's own
`PowerShell/EditorSyntax`, is unusable here — it ships a `.tmLanguage` plist, which
syntect cannot read as a syntax at any feature level.

The vendored set is consulted **last**, after the bundled set and the extras, so it
can only fill a hole and can never re-highlight a language that already resolved.

```powershell
# Both `powershell` and `ps1` resolve to this grammar.
$items = Get-ChildItem -Path "C:\logs" -Filter *.log
foreach ($f in $items) {
    Write-Host "reading $($f.Name)"
}
```

```ps1
Get-Process | Where-Object { $_.CPU -gt 10 } | Select-Object Name, CPU
```

## Fences that stay plain on purpose

`text` and `console` resolve to no syntax deliberately: a transcript or a chunk of
sample output is not code, so it must render unhighlighted *without* `taliesin check`
reporting it as an unknown language.

```text
the quick brown fox
jumps over the lazy dog
```

```console
$ taliesin build docs/guide
  built  _site/index.html
```

An unlabelled fence is likewise plain, and likewise silent.

```
no language token at all
```
